1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,524
You tap send. Somewhere else, a friend sees
your message. Let's

2
00:00:04,524 --> 00:00:09,048
slow that tiny journey down. Here is your
computer, here is

3
00:00:09,048 --> 00:00:13,571
your friend's, and here is a connection
between them. A network

4
00:00:13,571 --> 00:00:18,095
is simply connected devices that exchange
information. The message travels as

5
00:00:18,095 --> 00:00:22,619
small pieces of data, called packets. We will
draw one as

6
00:00:22,619 --> 00:00:27,143
a green box. What could make such a little
thing arrive

7
00:00:27,143 --> 00:00:31,667
late?

8
00:00:31,667 --> 00:00:36,867
Between our two computers sits a router: a
device that passes

9
00:00:36,867 --> 00:00:42,067
packets toward the next part of their journey.
Several packets may

10
00:00:42,067 --> 00:00:47,267
need the same outgoing connection, or link.
Picture a doorway that

11
00:00:47,267 --> 00:00:52,467
serves one packet after another. Arrive while
it is busy, and

12
00:00:52,467 --> 00:00:57,667
you wait. The router has memory for that
waiting data. That

13
00:00:57,667 --> 00:01:02,867
space is called a buffer; the waiting line is
a queue.

14
00:01:02,867 --> 00:01:07,581
Now watch the door. It cannot send all the
waiting data

15
00:01:07,581 --> 00:01:12,295
at once. Computers represent data using zeros
and ones. Each zero

16
00:01:12,295 --> 00:01:17,010
or one is a bit. A link's rate tells us how

17
00:01:17,010 --> 00:01:21,724
many bits it can send each second. More bits
per second

18
00:01:21,724 --> 00:01:26,438
means more data gets through. Think of
counting people leaving a

19
00:01:26,438 --> 00:01:31,152
cinema. It does not yet tell us how long one
person

20
00:01:31,152 --> 00:01:35,867
waited before reaching it.

21
00:01:35,867 --> 00:01:40,492
Start with an empty waiting room. Our green
packet arrives and

22
00:01:40,492 --> 00:01:45,117
takes its turn almost straight away. It still
needs time to

23
00:01:45,117 --> 00:01:49,742
be sent and to travel, so the journey is not
instant.

24
00:01:49,742 --> 00:01:54,367
But there is almost nothing waiting ahead of
it. Send another

25
00:01:54,367 --> 00:01:58,992
small message and the same thing happens. This
is the experience

26
00:01:58,992 --> 00:02:03,617
we want when pressing a button or talking on a
call:

27
00:02:03,617 --> 00:02:08,242
a small action gets a quick response. Now
let's make the

28
00:02:08,242 --> 00:02:12,867
connection busy.

29
00:02:12,867 --> 00:02:18,024
Imagine a large download arriving as lots of
blue packets. For

30
00:02:18,024 --> 00:02:23,181
a while, they reach our doorway faster than it
can send

31
00:02:23,181 --> 00:02:28,338
them onward. One leaves, but more join. A line
builds up.

32
00:02:28,338 --> 00:02:33,495
The exit keeps working, which looks great on a
download counter.

33
00:02:33,495 --> 00:02:38,652
Yet look inside the waiting room. That pile is
growing. We

34
00:02:38,652 --> 00:02:43,810
have changed the amount of waiting without
making the doorway any

35
00:02:43,810 --> 00:02:48,967
narrower. This is the clue that a speed test
can miss.

36
00:02:48,967 --> 00:02:53,533
Send the green message again. It is just as
small as

37
00:02:53,533 --> 00:02:58,100
before. But this time, blue packets got here
first. In our

38
00:02:58,100 --> 00:03:02,667
simple first-come, first-served line, green
must wait behind them. Being small

39
00:03:02,667 --> 00:03:07,233
helps with its own turn at the door; it does
not

40
00:03:07,233 --> 00:03:11,800
erase everybody else's turn. A download can
keep moving while your

41
00:03:11,800 --> 00:03:16,367
call feels late. Two things are happening at
once: plenty of

42
00:03:16,367 --> 00:03:20,933
data is leaving, and one particular packet is
spending too long

43
00:03:20,933 --> 00:03:25,500
waiting.

44
00:03:25,500 --> 00:03:30,179
Here is a tempting fix: make the waiting room
bigger. Surely

45
00:03:30,179 --> 00:03:34,858
more space means a better connection? Watch
carefully. The room expands,

46
00:03:34,858 --> 00:03:39,538
but the door stays exactly the same. It still
sends the

47
00:03:39,538 --> 00:03:44,217
same number of bits each second. Extra memory
lets more packets

48
00:03:44,217 --> 00:03:48,896
wait inside. It does not make their turns
finish sooner. A

49
00:03:48,896 --> 00:03:53,575
bigger room can be useful for a short rush.
But if

50
00:03:53,575 --> 00:03:58,254
we keep it full, we have built a longer wait,
not

51
00:03:58,254 --> 00:04:02,933
a faster exit.

52
00:04:02,933 --> 00:04:07,667
We can measure that wait using an everyday
idea. If ten

53
00:04:07,667 --> 00:04:12,400
people are ahead of you and two leave each
second, clearing

54
00:04:12,400 --> 00:04:17,133
the line takes five seconds. Data works the
same way in

55
00:04:17,133 --> 00:04:21,867
our model. Take the number of bits ahead and
divide by

56
00:04:21,867 --> 00:04:26,600
the number leaving each second. We call those
quantities Q and

57
00:04:26,600 --> 00:04:31,333
R, so the shorthand is Q divided by R. The
letters

58
00:04:31,333 --> 00:04:36,067
add nothing mysterious. They stand for how
much is waiting and

59
00:04:36,067 --> 00:04:40,800
how fast it leaves.

60
00:04:40,800 --> 00:04:45,592
Before using real numbers, we need one small
unit check. A

61
00:04:45,592 --> 00:04:50,383
byte is a group of eight bits. Files are often
measured

62
00:04:50,383 --> 00:04:55,175
in bytes, while connection rates are often
given in bits per

63
00:04:55,175 --> 00:04:59,967
second. Our waiting data is one megabyte.
Here, mega means one

64
00:04:59,967 --> 00:05:04,758
million, so that is one million bytes. Expand
each byte into

65
00:05:04,758 --> 00:05:09,550
eight bits and we get eight million bits. Now
both our

66
00:05:09,550 --> 00:05:14,342
waiting amount and our outgoing rate can use
the same unit.

67
00:05:14,342 --> 00:05:19,133
The division will make sense.

68
00:05:19,133 --> 00:05:23,867
Our exit sends twenty million bits each
second. There are eight

69
00:05:23,867 --> 00:05:28,600
million bits ahead of green. Divide eight
million by twenty million:

70
00:05:28,600 --> 00:05:33,333
zero point four seconds. That is four hundred
milliseconds, because a

71
00:05:33,333 --> 00:05:38,067
second contains one thousand milliseconds.
Watch eight blocks leave; each block

72
00:05:38,067 --> 00:05:42,800
represents one million bits, not one normal
packet. Every block adds

73
00:05:42,800 --> 00:05:47,533
fifty milliseconds. By the eighth, four
hundred milliseconds have passed before

74
00:05:47,533 --> 00:05:52,267
green can start its own turn. A short message
can inherit

75
00:05:52,267 --> 00:05:57,000
a surprisingly long delay.

76
00:05:57,000 --> 00:06:01,679
That four hundred milliseconds is only the
wait at this doorway.

77
00:06:01,679 --> 00:06:06,358
Signals also need time to travel. Devices need
time to examine

78
00:06:06,358 --> 00:06:11,038
data, and putting a packet onto a link takes
time too.

79
00:06:11,038 --> 00:06:15,717
A reply must make a journey back. Those other
delays have

80
00:06:15,717 --> 00:06:20,396
not vanished; we simply left them out while
measuring this one

81
00:06:20,396 --> 00:06:25,075
queue. The important point is visible in the
bar: a large

82
00:06:25,075 --> 00:06:29,754
waiting line can add a big chunk of delay to
an

83
00:06:29,754 --> 00:06:34,433
otherwise working connection.

84
00:06:34,433 --> 00:06:39,058
Perhaps the line will fix itself once arrivals
slow down. Let's

85
00:06:39,058 --> 00:06:43,683
test that. One packet leaves, and one new
packet joins. Another

86
00:06:43,683 --> 00:06:48,308
leaves, another joins. Count the boxes. The
line stays the same

87
00:06:48,308 --> 00:06:52,933
length. Equal arrivals and departures stop the
queue from growing, but

88
00:06:52,933 --> 00:06:57,558
they do not make an existing queue disappear.
To shrink it,

89
00:06:57,558 --> 00:07:02,183
more data must leave than arrives for a while.
The door

90
00:07:02,183 --> 00:07:06,808
can work perfectly steadily while every new
message still finds a

91
00:07:06,808 --> 00:07:11,433
line ahead.

92
00:07:11,433 --> 00:07:16,167
Now try a brief rush followed by a quiet
moment. Blue

93
00:07:16,167 --> 00:07:20,900
packets arrive together, wait briefly, and
drain away. When green arrives

94
00:07:20,900 --> 00:07:25,633
later, the doorway is clear again. That is
useful buffering: the

95
00:07:25,633 --> 00:07:30,367
room absorbed uneven arrivals. The problem is
a line that stays

96
00:07:30,367 --> 00:07:35,100
large, delaying message after message. So we
should not treat every

97
00:07:35,100 --> 00:07:39,833
waiting packet as a disaster. A short queue
during a rush

98
00:07:39,833 --> 00:07:44,567
and a standing queue all evening are very
different experiences, even

99
00:07:44,567 --> 00:07:49,300
with the same doorway.

100
00:07:49,300 --> 00:07:53,979
Our waiting room is not endless. Fill every
space, then bring

101
00:07:53,979 --> 00:07:58,658
in another packet. There may be nowhere to put
it, so

102
00:07:58,658 --> 00:08:03,338
the device discards it. That is packet loss.
Waiting and being

103
00:08:03,338 --> 00:08:08,017
discarded are different problems: one packet
arrives late, another does not

104
00:08:08,017 --> 00:08:12,696
arrive through this attempt. A larger room may
delay the moment

105
00:08:12,696 --> 00:08:17,375
it fills, while also allowing longer waits.
Real devices can manage

106
00:08:17,375 --> 00:08:22,054
their queues more carefully. Our drawing
explains why simply adding storage

107
00:08:22,054 --> 00:08:26,733
is not enough.

108
00:08:26,733 --> 00:08:31,467
Compare these two doors. Both send data at the
same rate.

109
00:08:31,467 --> 00:08:36,200
The top has a long standing queue; the bottom
has a

110
00:08:36,200 --> 00:08:40,933
shorter one. Watch the blue packets already
ahead leave first in

111
00:08:40,933 --> 00:08:45,667
both rows. Green gets its turn much earlier in
the shorter

112
00:08:45,667 --> 00:08:50,400
line. It has not jumped the queue, and the
door has

113
00:08:50,400 --> 00:08:55,133
not become faster. This is what better queue
management aims to

114
00:08:55,133 --> 00:08:59,867
improve: keep the connection useful while
avoiding a big pile of

115
00:08:59,867 --> 00:09:04,600
data that waits unnecessarily.

116
00:09:04,600 --> 00:09:09,392
Now the name makes sense. Bufferbloat is
excessive delay caused by

117
00:09:09,392 --> 00:09:14,183
too much data sitting in buffers when a
connection is busy.

118
00:09:14,183 --> 00:09:18,975
Throughput means how much data gets through;
delay means how long

119
00:09:18,975 --> 00:09:23,767
something takes. A connection can have good
throughput and still respond

120
00:09:23,767 --> 00:09:28,558
slowly. Bufferbloat is one possible cause of
lag, not the answer

121
00:09:28,558 --> 00:09:33,350
to every connection problem. Next time a
download looks fast but

122
00:09:33,350 --> 00:09:38,142
a conversation feels late, remember our green
box. How much is

123
00:09:38,142 --> 00:09:42,933
waiting in front of it?
